# T8.9: every gesture, against the local SQL Server, with keys only to type
# and to show what a click did. Run at 120x40 and 80x24 by qa.sh, each with a
# state directory of its own so the pad starts empty. Everything is found
# with `on <text>` so it holds at both sizes, up to the last section, whose
# gestures (a drag, a scrollbar, a seam) only take X Y and which therefore
# resizes to one known layout first.
#
# `wait 500` keeps two clicks on one spot from being a double-click.

# The placeholder is a button, and the footer's connection state toggles.
click on [ Connect ]
wait busy
expect ● connected
expect ▾ dbo
click on ● connected
expect ○ disconnected
expect [ Connect ]
click on ○ disconnected
wait busy
expect ● connected

# A tab is shown by clicking it, and a disconnected one connects.
click on 2 local-oracle
wait busy
expect local-oracle connected
expect-not [ Connect ]
click on 1 local-mssql
expect ● connected
expect ▾ dbo

# A click focuses the pane it lands in, which the help chip then names; a
# click beside the help closes it and reaches nothing under it.
click on nothing has run yet
click on ? Help
expect Help · Results
click on ▶ Run
expect-not Help ·
expect nothing has run yet
click on ? Help
click on ×
expect-not Help ·

# The tree: a glyph toggles, the wheel scrolls without moving the focus.
click on ▾ dbo
expect ▸ dbo
click on ▸ bench
wait busy
expect ▾ bench
click on ▸ Tables
wait busy
expect ▸ customers
click on ▸ customers
wait busy
expect ▾ customers
click on ▸ all_types
wait busy
expect ▾ all_types
scroll down on ▾ all_types
expect-not ▾ bench
scroll up on ▾ all_types
expect ▾ bench
click on ▾ all_types
click on ▾ customers
expect ▸ all_types
expect ▸ customers

# A click on a name moves the cursor there, and a help row is its key: Enter
# on the table the click chose. A double-click is two clicks on one row, so
# the wait keeps this one from pairing with the glyph's.
wait 500
click on customers
click on ? Help
expect Help · Objects
click on select from it
expect-not Help ·
expect select top 100 * from bench.customers

# A double-click on a procedure is Enter: its source.
click on ▸ Procedures
wait busy
double-click on sp_customer_o
wait busy
expect Source · bench.sp_customer_orders

# The filter button, and the × that clears it.
click on / Filter
type cust
expect Objects /cust
expect-not ▸ orders
click on ×
expect-not Objects /cust
expect ▸ orders

# The pad: a click places the cursor, Run runs the statement it is in, All
# runs them all.
click on select top 100
key End
key Enter
key Enter
type select 1 as one
click on ▶▶ All
wait busy
expect 2 statements, 2 result sets
click on select top 100
wait 500
click on ▶ Run
wait busy
expect Results · 50 rows
expect-not 2 statements

# A footer hint is a button.
click on Ctrl-T next tab
expect nothing has run yet
click on 1 local-mssql
expect Results · 50 rows

# The grid: the wheel scrolls rows, the sideways wheel columns.
expect │   1  Zo
scroll down on Zoë Bauer
expect-not │   1  Zo
expect │   4  José Álvarez
scroll up on José Álvarez
expect │   1  Zo
expect id   name
scroll right on Zoë Bauer
expect-not id   name
expect name              email
scroll left on Zoë Bauer
expect id   name

# A click selects a cell, which Enter then inspects; a click beside the
# inspector closes it and does not reach the tab under it.
click on customer3@example.com
key Enter
expect email · varchar
click on 2 local-oracle
expect-not email · varchar
expect ● connected

# A double-click inspects, and the inspector's × closes it.
double-click on Ægir Nilsen
expect name · nvarchar · 11 chars
click on ×
expect-not name · nvarchar

# A header click sorts by that column: up, down, then as the rows came.
click on name
expect ▲  email
wait 500
click on ▲
expect ▼  email
wait 500
click on ▼
expect id   name              email
expect │   1  Zo

# A right-click selects the cell and opens the pane's menu. A click beside it
# only closes it; a click on an entry is its key.
right-click on customer1@example.com
expect inspect the cell
click on 1 local-mssql
expect-not inspect the cell
right-click on customer2@example.com
click on inspect the cell
expect email · varchar · 21 chars
click on 1 local-mssql
expect-not email · varchar

# The Export button opens the prompt; a click beside it gives up, the way
# Esc does, and the tree under it is left alone.
click on Export
expect Export to:
click on ▸ dbo
expect-not Export to:
expect ▸ dbo

# The wheel over the pad scrolls it and leaves the cursor where it can be seen.
click on select 1 as one
key End
key Enter
key Enter
key Enter
key Enter
key Enter
key Enter
key Enter
key Enter
key Enter
key Enter
key Enter
key Enter
key Enter
key Enter
type select 2 as two
expect select 2 as two
scroll up on select 2 as two
expect-not select 2 as two

# Hover paints; its style is asserted in src/ui/tests/mouse.rs, and here only
# that it changes nothing.
hover on ▶ Run
expect ▶ Run

# Gestures that take coordinates, at the README's size: Objects is x 0-29,
# the seam between it and the right column x 30, Scratch rows 1-11 with its
# bottom border (the other seam) on row 11, and the grid's scrollbar x 99.
resize 100x30
expect ╭ Objects ──────── / Filter ─╮╭ Scratch
expect │   1  Zo

# The track below the thumb is PageDown; dragging the thumb to the bottom
# shows the last row.
click 99 25
expect-not │   1  Zo
drag 99 16 99 27
expect customer50@example.com

# Dragging a seam moves the border between the panes; a double-click puts
# 30/40 back.
drag 30 5 50 5
expect ╭ Objects ───────────────── ⟳ Reload ─ / Filter ─╮╭ Scratch
double-click 50 5
expect ╭ Objects ──────── / Filter ─╮╭ Scratch
drag 60 11 60 18
expect │                            │╰──
expect │     ▸ orders               ││
double-click 60 18
expect │     ▸ orders               │╰──
expect-not │                            │╰──

# A drag across pad text selects it, and Ctrl-C copies exactly that: the
# word `select` on line 1, once the wheel has brought line 1 back.
scroll up 50 5
scroll up 50 5
scroll up 50 5
scroll up 50 5
scroll up 50 5
drag 35 2 41 2
key Ctrl-C
expect copied 6 characters

key Ctrl-Q
